/**
\page nodes Nodes

The data model of the Yoyo application consists of nodes organized in a tree structure (thus the
name).

There are a variety of nodes defined already in Yoyo. You can find them in src/dataModel/basicNodes.

\section nodes_fundamental Fundamental nodes
Fundamental nodes are for the biggest part directly derived from the node_base class and some of
them are expected to be in the hierarchy once and once only.

They have one thing in common: they are not meant to be specialized. They are available from the
fundamental node factory, which is not exposed to the plugin framework.

The following types of fundamental nodes are defined:

|node|multiplexity|   |
|:---|:--:|:--|
|configuration     |1   |The configuration is the root node of the complete data-model.|
|data_root         |1   |This node acts as root for all data nodes and always is the first child of the configuration node. Only data nodes are allowed as children, whether direct or indirect.|
|gui_root          |1   |This node acts as root for all gui nodes and always is the second child of the configuration node. Only gui nodes are allowed as children, whether direct or indirect.|
|configuration_data|1   |This leaf node contains some properties for the configuration, which are user manageable. It is always the third child of the configuration node.|
|protocol_root     |0..*|The user can add protocols to the configuration. This node acts as root node for all nodes belonging to one protocol, so each protocol has one protocol_root node.|
|communication_root|0..*|The user can add communication interfaces to the configuration. This node acts as root node for all nodes belonging to one communication interface.|
|message_handler   |0..*|This node needs to be present as a child of a communication_root node for the communication to work. It is an inner node; the children are of type message.|
|message           |0..*|This node defines a message to be processed by the communication interface. It is an inner node hosting message_field children.|
|message_field     |0..*|The message_field node is a leaf node connecting the message to a data node which can act as a source or a sink for the communication.|

\section nodes_data Data nodes
The data nodes are somewhat special: they have a type and they can be connected to other node types.
Connections between other node types (gui with gui or gui with protocol etc) are not allowed, as
well as connections between data nodes. Connected nodes can transfer data to data nodes, which then
distribute the data to all other connected nodes.

Even though offered to the plugin framework, it should not be necessary to add new data nodes; the
set of nodes provided by Yoyo is meant to be complete:

|node|type|   |
|:---|:--:|:--|
|bit_node|BIT|Leaf-node, contains one bit (a boolean)|
|bitfield_node|BITFIELD|Leaf-node, contains variable length data; must be a child of a register_node.|
|string_node|STRING|Leaf-node, contains a string|
|float_node|FLOAT|Leaf-node, contains a floating point value|
|uint8node|UINT8|Leaf-node, contains an 8 bit unsigned integer|
|uint16node|UINT16|Leaf-node, contains an 16 bit unsigned integer|
|uint32node|UINT32|Leaf-node, contains an 32 bit unsigned integer|
|uint64node|UINT64|Leaf-node, contains an 64 bit unsigned integer|
|int8node|INT8|Leaf-node, contains an 8 bit signed integer|
|int16node|INT16|Leaf-node, contains an 16 bit signed integer|
|int32node|INT32|Leaf-node, contains an 32 bit signed integer|
|int64node|INT64|Leaf-node, contains an 64 bit signed integer|
|register_node|REGISTER|Inner node, can host bit-fields. register_nodes and their bitfield_node children share the same memory|
|data_group|CUSTOM|Inner node, can host any data node except bitfield_node|

Data nodes are available through the data node factory.

\section nodes_gui Gui nodes
Gui nodes are available through the gui node factory.

Some basic gui nodes are defined already without using a plugin. This includes:
- Basic groups
- PushButton
- CheckBox
- ComboBox
- LineEdit
- Spacer
- TabWidget
- Label
- Image
- SpinBox
- TextEdit
- LCD Number
- Sliders

\section nodes_protocol Protocol nodes
Protocol nodes are available through the protocol node factory.
\section nodes_communication Communication nodes
Communication nodes are available through the communication node factory.
\section nodes_user User defined nodes
User defined nodes are leaf nodes, which contain a node tree. They can be installed to any of the
previously discussed node factories (apart from the fundamental node factory, which is not open for
extension).
The UDN is in principle a single class of node, representing a sub-tree previously defined by the
user.
\warning The UDNs are in concept phase. There is no implementation yet!
\subsection nodes_user_create Type creation
In the Browser Widget, a right click on any group-like node (data_group or register_node for data
nodes, group for gui nodes) will show in the context menu an option to create an UDN from the sub
tree, starting at that node.

On activating this option, a dialog will pop up. This dialog shows all node in the sub-tree with
their respective properties (no changes possible, just a list). The user can then decide, which of
these properties to expose as property of the UDN. For each property, the user needs to specify a
documentation (default is the documentation of the forwarded property).
\note All connectivity related properties must be exposed.

The user may choose to define the UDN globally or locally (configuration specific).

The UDN definition consists then of the serialization of the subtree and the list of forwarded
properties. A type identifier is generated at this point.

The UDN creation function is then added to the respective factory.

\subsection nodes_user_instatiate Type instantiation
As all other nodes, the UDN is created by the respective node factory using the provided creation
function.

For each node category there is one UDN base node, which derives from the base node for the
respective node factory to be accepted. UDN itself is not derived from node_base (or QObject for
this matter), but is a template class to be used for multiple inheritance as an add-on for the
respective node class. \see https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/memory/enable_shared_from_this

The UDN itself is a leaf node, so it doesn't have direct children using the interface provided by
node_base. Instead it contains an instance of the sub-tree it was generated from, which is not
part of the configuration tree. All exposed properties are added to the UDN using dynamic
properties (https://doc.qt.io/qt-5/properties.html#dynamic-properties).
\warning Dynamic properties don't come with a change notifier, so the connectivity manager must
connect to the propertyChanged signal of the UDN to be able to perform connections between data
nodes and other nodes.

For gui nodes, the widget of the root of the sub-tree is added to the UDN's widget as only child.

\subsection nodes_user_storage Storing UDN in configuration file
UDNs can be stored locally in a configuration file. This way, they are part of the configuration
node, defined as a private property.

When loading a configuration file, they are added to the respective node factories and removed at
closing a configuration.

\subsection nodes_user_load Loading UDN types
Globally stored UDNs are stored in dedicated files and can be loaded or unloaded using the Plugin
Manager.
*/
